window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][36] = "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Meta's consumer products are free to users and funded by advertising, and its business tools involve advertiser pricing <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Meta Form 10-K for 2025]</code>. Candidates commonly report questions on monetizing a messaging or social product <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: separate user value from advertiser value, and name the experience guardrails on ads.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft sells productivity and cloud products by subscription and has offered AI assistants as add-ons and in bundles <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Microsoft 365 Copilot at 30 dollars per user per month from 1 November 2023 (Directions on Microsoft)]</code>. Candidates commonly report packaging, bundling and enterprise-pricing questions <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: reason about add-on versus bundle, seat-based pricing with usage, and enterprise buying (C25.6).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon runs a paid membership that bundles delivery, video and other benefits <code>Public</code> <code>[V: FTC action and settlement]</code>, and has faced scrutiny of enrollment and cancellation practices (C10.10). Candidates commonly report questions on membership benefits and seller pricing <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: compute the cost per member of a benefit against its effect on retention and spend, and design honest sign-up and cancellation.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple sells premium hardware and a growing set of services <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Apple Form 10-K for fiscal 2025]</code>. Candidates commonly report premium-pricing and services-attach questions <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: connect device margin, services attach and retention (Worked example 1), and justify premium positioning with craft and ecosystem value.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix is a subscription service that introduced an ad-supported tier and tightened account sharing in recent years <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix ad tier launched 3 November 2022; extra-member fee introduced 2023 (Fast Company)]</code>. Candidates commonly report questions on tiers, price changes and ad tiers <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: reason about tier fences, cannibalization of the premium plan, ad load and retention.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Stripe's payments pricing is usage-based, a percentage plus a fixed amount per transaction, with custom pricing for larger users <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Stripe pricing page: percentage plus fixed fee per transaction, custom pricing for large volume]</code>. Candidates report questions on developer-product pricing <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: choose a value metric that scales with the customer's success, and discuss volume discounts and custom pricing.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c20-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Pricing with no customer</td>\n<td>&quot;I'd charge $10&quot;</td>\n<td>Start from the customer and value; floor, reference, ceiling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Cost-plus as the method</td>\n<td>&quot;Cost plus 30%&quot;</td>\n<td>Cost is the floor; value sets the ceiling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Copying a competitor</td>\n<td>&quot;Same as theirs&quot;</td>\n<td>Position and value first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Trusting survey intent</td>\n<td>&quot;62% said they'd buy&quot;</td>\n<td>Use as relative; test on real traffic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Wrong value metric</td>\n<td>Seats for a usage-cost product</td>\n<td>Metric that tracks value and cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>&quot;Go viral&quot; as strategy</td>\n<td>No k, no cycle time</td>\n<td>Compute k and the multiplier</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Blended CAC</td>\n<td>One CAC for all channels</td>\n<td>CAC by channel; fully loaded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>LTV without churn evidence</td>\n<td>A constant 5% assumed</td>\n<td>Cohort churn; sensitivity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>A launch with no success measure</td>\n<td>A task list</td>\n<td>Metric, guardrails, decision rule</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Ignoring existing customers</td>\n<td>A price change with no plan</td>\n<td>Grandfathering and communication</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c20-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter.</p>\n<p><strong>Anchoring</strong> (<code>MM-anchoring</code>). The first number a person sees shapes their sense of value <code>[V: Tversky and Kahneman, Judgment under Uncertainty: Heuristics and Biases, Science 185(4157), 1974, pp. 1124 to 1131 (adjustment from an anchor)]</code>. A visible higher tier makes the middle plan look reasonable; a competitor's price frames yours. Use it honestly in packaging, and test it.</p>\n<p><strong>Compounding</strong> (<code>MM-compounding</code>). Growth that feeds itself builds on its own results. A loop with k near 1 and a short cycle time compounds; one with k of 0.3 and a long cycle does not. Compute before betting on it.</p>\n<p><strong>Marginal thinking</strong> (<code>MM-marginal-thinking</code>). Decide by the change an action causes, not by averages: the contribution of the next customer or the next task, not the average margin. It explains why heavy users can be unprofitable at an average price (Worked example 3).</p>\n<p><strong>Adverse selection</strong> (<code>MM-adverse-selection</code>). When a price or offer attracts the customers who benefit most and cost most, the average customer is worse than expected <code>[V: Akerlof, Quarterly Journal of Economics 84(3), August 1970, pp. 488 to 500, the founding paper on quality uncertainty; the insurance-style wording here is the usual textbook form]</code>. Unlimited plans attract heavy users; a delivery subscription attracts frequent orderers. Model the segment that joins.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineering.</strong> Pricing and packaging become entitlements, billing, metering and migration; a pricing change can be a large project. Involve engineering early, and ask what a usage meter, a plan change or a grandfathered price costs to build and run.</li>\n<li><strong>Design.</strong> The pricing page, the paywall and the upgrade prompt are product surfaces: clarity and honesty matter (C10.10), and complexity costs conversion. Test the page like any other.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> Price tests, willingness-to-pay analyses, LTV and churn models need careful design (C14): interference between prices, novelty and selection effects. Agree the metric, the guardrails and the duration up front.</li>\n<li><strong>Finance, legal and sales.</strong> Finance reviews margins and forecasts; legal reviews terms, consent, auto-renewal rules and regional requirements; sales needs a price list and a clear story. Bring each in before the announcement.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c20-checklist-before-you-propose-a-price-or-a-launch\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: before you propose a price or a launch</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Have I named the customer, the problem and the alternative?</li>\n<li>Do I have a cost floor, a reference and a value estimate, each with a number?</li>\n<li>Have I chosen the model, the value metric and the tiers, and said what each tier is for?</li>\n<li>Is there a test plan with a holdout, a metric and guardrails?</li>\n<li>Have I computed CAC (by channel), LTV, payback and a sensitivity grid?</li>\n<li>For a loop, have I computed k, the multiplier and the cycle time?</li>\n<li>Does the launch plan have a tier, a checklist, a timeline, success criteria and rollback triggers?</li>\n<li>Have I involved product marketing, sales, support, finance and legal, and planned for existing customers?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c20-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c20-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C20-01 · The add-on priced at cost plus thirty percent · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented (invented).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> A business-software team builds a reporting add-on for its analytics product. The cost to serve is $9 per seat per month. Finance suggests a cost-plus price of $12 (cost plus about 30%), and the team prepares to launch at $12.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A quarter to launch; 1,000 seats of target customers in an early-access group; no pricing research done; a PM new to pricing.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Interviews with 15 customers suggest the add-on saves each analyst three to four hours a month, which they value at $30 to $50 an hour. A price-sensitivity survey of the early-access group (invented) suggests that 80% would buy at $12, 52% at $35 and 25% at $60.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> As an observable problem: &quot;The price is set from cost, while customers' willingness to pay is several times higher, so the team may leave most of the value uncaptured.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> Applying the pricing worksheet (FW-C20-03): the cost floor is $9; the reference (what customers use now, a manual process and two competing tools priced at about $30 to $40) is higher; the value ceiling is on the order of $90 to $200 a month per analyst (three to four hours at $30 to $50). Stated intent overstates purchase, so the survey is used for relative comparison. Revenue and margin per 1,000 seats: at $12, 800 buy: revenue $9,600 and margin $3 × 800 = $2,400. At $35, 520 buy: revenue $18,200 and margin $26 × 520 = $13,520. At $60, 250 buy: revenue $15,000 and margin $51 × 250 = $12,750. The $35 price yields about 1.9 times the revenue and 5.6 times the margin of $12.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Launch at $12 (cost plus)</td>\n<td>Adoption and simplicity</td>\n<td>Leaves most of the value uncaptured; hard to raise later</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Launch at $35 after a live test of $29, $35 and $45 on new accounts</td>\n<td>Revenue and margin with evidence</td>\n<td>Slower launch; possible lower adoption</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Launch at $60</td>\n<td>Margin per seat</td>\n<td>Lower volume; risk of positioning as a luxury</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B costs a few weeks and yields evidence; A is simple and gives away value; C may be too high for the evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Choose B: test $29, $35 and $45 on a holdout of new accounts for four weeks, measuring conversion, revenue per account and 60-day retention, with a guardrail on support contacts; set the packaging as an add-on with a free 14-day trial.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario, the $35 price converted 48% of trial accounts and had the highest revenue per account; $45 converted 33%; $29 converted 53% with lower revenue; retention at 60 days was similar across arms (invented). The team launched at $35.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If $45 had converted nearly as well as $35, the team would have moved up and tested again. If 60-day retention had fallen at higher prices, the team would have favored the lower price. The lesson: cost is a floor, value sets the ceiling, and a test settles the price.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c20-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C20-02 · Adobe, 2013: from a boxed suite to a $49.99-a-month subscription · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): Adobe's press release of 6 May 2013 (primary; its full text was read as reproduced on a technology news site that cites the adobe.com original); Adobe's quarterly report for the period ended 31 May 2013, its earnings releases for the third and fourth quarters of fiscal 2013 (filed as exhibits with the US Securities and Exchange Commission), and its annual reports for fiscal 2011, 2012 and 2014 (all primary); and two secondary reports of the list prices of the boxed suite, whose editions are not identical. The analysis is the author's. Figures and prices were re-read against these sources in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> On 6 May 2013 Adobe announced that it would stop developing new versions of its boxed Creative Suite software and would deliver its creative tools through Creative Cloud, a subscription. The release stated that individual Creative Cloud membership was $49.99 a month based on an annual membership, that existing owners of CS3 to CS5.5 would get their first year at $29.99 a month, that students and teachers would pay $29.99 a month, and that teams would pay $69.99 a month per seat; it added that promotional pricing was available for some customers, including CS6 users, and that Adobe would keep selling CS6 and had no plans for future releases of Creative Suite <code>[V: Adobe press release, 6 May 2013]</code>. Adobe's fiscal 2011 annual report says it announced in October 2011 a major new offering to deploy and license its creative tools, Creative Cloud, marketed as a subscription and expected to be available in 2012 <code>[V: annual report, fiscal 2011]</code>, and the release said the service had more than half a million paid members and well over two million free members, about a year after its launch <code>[V: press release]</code>. In its quarterly report for the period ended 31 May 2013, Adobe reported that its Digital Media revenue had decreased by $142.6 million in the quarter, compared with the same quarter a year earlier, as it transitioned toward subscriptions, with subscription revenue recognized ratably over the term. It exited its third fiscal quarter of 2013 with about 1.03 million paid Creative Cloud subscriptions and a Creative annualized recurring revenue of $546 million, and its fourth quarter with about 1.44 million subscriptions and a Creative annualized recurring revenue of $768 million <code>[V: earnings releases, 2013]</code>. The documents contain one unexplained difference: the fourth-quarter release states an increase of 402 thousand subscriptions over the end of the third quarter, while the two reported totals differ by 408 thousand, and the filings read do not say why. Adobe's annual report for fiscal 2014 shows net income of $832.8 million in fiscal 2012, $290.0 million in fiscal 2013 and $268.4 million in fiscal 2014 <code>[V: annual report, fiscal 2014]</code>; the filings read here do not apportion the fall among its causes.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A flagship product line for creative professionals (the release calls Creative Cloud the company's flagship offering for creatives); a large installed base of perpetual-license customers; revenue recognized over the term under a subscription, where a boxed license was recognized when sold; and a risk that existing customers would feel they were being forced (an inference: the sources read do not describe customer reaction).</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As reported: the announcement and price points; the quarterly revenue decrease in Digital Media; the subscription counts and annualized recurring revenue at the end of the third and fourth quarters; net income for fiscal 2012 to 2014.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>The author's reading.</em> Adobe wanted a more predictable, growing revenue base and a closer relationship with customers, at the cost of a revenue dip and the risk of alienating existing buyers. As an observable problem: &quot;How to change the pricing model of a product with a large installed base without losing its customers or its investors' confidence.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's analysis, through this chapter's tools.</em></p>\n<p><em>Revenue timing, the verified mechanism.</em> A perpetual license is recognized as revenue when sold; a subscription is recognized ratably over its term. Adobe's annual report for fiscal 2012 says that the success of Creative Cloud adoption lowered reported revenue for that reason, and its quarterly report for the period ended 31 May 2013 attributes the $142.6 million fall in Digital Media revenue to adoption of the subscription offerings <code>[V: annual report, fiscal 2012; quarterly report]</code>. The arithmetic of the price: $49.99 a month is $599.88 a year. Reported list prices of the boxed suite varied by edition, about $1,299 for one and $2,599 for the Master Collection <code>[V: two secondary reports, 2013 and 2024]</code>, so a subscription takes about 26 to 52 months (1,299 ÷ 49.99 and 2,599 ÷ 49.99) to collect the same total as one box. How this compares with what customers paid over time depends on how often they upgraded and at what upgrade price, which the sources read do not show, so I make no claim that revenue per customer fell or rose. The case for the change had to rest on more customers, longer retention and predictability (an inference).</p>\n<p><em>Metrics that matter in a subscription.</em> Revenue recognized over time hides progress in the short run; the metrics the company emphasized were the number of paid subscriptions and annualized recurring revenue, which moved quickly: Creative ARR rose from $546 million to $768 million in a single quarter. This is the lesson of choosing the right metric (C05) when changing a model: the income statement lagged, and leading indicators showed the change working.</p>\n<p><em>Packaging and the value metric.</em> The release shows tiering by customer type: individuals ($49.99 on an annual membership), owners of earlier suites (a discounted first year at $29.99), students and teachers ($29.99) and teams ($69.99 a seat), with promotional pricing for some customers <code>[V: press release]</code>. The discounted first year for owners is a transition tool (grandfathering in effect): it eased the move for existing buyers. Whether any lower-priced plan widened the base is not shown in the sources read.</p>\n<p><em>Why it could work for Adobe (inference).</em> The release says that focusing development on Creative Cloud would speed the rate of innovation, so a subscription could deliver continuing value and a recurring fee matched a recurring benefit; whether customers had few close alternatives is not shown in the sources read. For a product without continuing value, the same move could lose customers.</p>\n<p><em>What the evidence does not show.</em> The share of customers lost or the effect of competitors' promotions; the precise reasoning inside the company; and how much of the later growth is due to the pricing change rather than to market growth and product investment. The case supports an analysis of the economics and the transition, not a claim that subscriptions always win.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction of choices the company could have faced; not a record of its deliberations.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Keep perpetual licenses and offer a subscription as a choice</td>\n<td>Lower risk of alienating customers</td>\n<td>Two models to run; slower shift in predictability; less pressure to convert</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Move new development to subscription only, with a discounted first year for existing customers and promotional pricing</td>\n<td>Recurring revenue, a wider base, predictability</td>\n<td>A revenue dip; customer backlash; investor skepticism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Move gradually over many years</td>\n<td>Smoothness</td>\n<td>Slower; competitors and expectations may move first</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B accepts a near-term revenue and earnings decline for a bigger, steadier base; A keeps the old revenue and the old problems; C delays the choice.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, Adobe chose B on 6 May 2013.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Reported: Creative Cloud subscriptions passed one million within the third quarter of fiscal 2013 and reached about 1.44 million by the end of the year, and Adobe's annual report for fiscal 2014 shows net income falling from $832.8 million in fiscal 2012 to $290.0 million in fiscal 2013 and $268.4 million in fiscal 2014 <code>[V: earnings releases; annual report, fiscal 2014]</code>. Longer-run results come from company reports beyond the sources read here and are not claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> A company with weaker lock-in might have lost customers to alternatives. A reader should avoid hindsight: the decision looked risky then, with a revenue gap forecast and customer anger. The method to carry away: compare revenue recognition before and after, choose the leading metrics for the new model, use transition pricing for existing customers, and check whether your product's switching costs and continuous value could support the change.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Nine questions. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code> (composite candidates), and every figure is invented (invented). Each ends with follow-ups and answers. Where a real company's product is the setting, the answer reasons from public information and states no internal fact. The answers are fuller than a first pass in a real round.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c20-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C20-01 · Beginner · strategy-gtm · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What is a go-to-market plan, and what is a PM's role in it?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's the plan for launching a product with marketing.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the elements: customer, value, positioning, price, channels, launch, metrics.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes motions, assigns ownership across functions, ties the plan to a success measure, and gives an example.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;A go-to-market plan is how a product reaches its target customers and wins them: who they are and what problem they have, what we say to them, what it costs and how it is packaged, how they find and buy it, how we launch, and how we'll know it's working. The motion varies: product-led for a consumer app or developer tool, sales-led for enterprise, partner-led where a platform brings the customers.</p>\n<p>&quot;The PM owns the customer problem, the value and the product, and works closely with product marketing on positioning and launch, with sales and support on enablement, and with finance on price. The PM usually co-owns the positioning and pricing decisions and carries the launch. For example, when we launched an expense-capture feature for small-business owners, I defined the target (owners tracking expenses in spreadsheets), the message (receipts captured automatically), the in-product launch and the success measure: the share of active owners who capture ten receipts in two weeks, with support contacts as a guardrail.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> The elements; motions; ownership; a success measure.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Who decides the price? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;It depends on the company, usually a pricing owner or leadership with finance, informed by the PM and product marketing. I'd bring willingness-to-pay evidence, the cost floor and a test plan, and agree who decides before the work starts.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if product marketing and the PM disagree on the message? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd test both with customers: which version do they repeat, and what do they think the product does? The evidence settles most disagreements, and where it doesn't, we agree who decides and move on.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c20-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C20-02 · Beginner · strategy-gtm · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Write a positioning statement for a new note-taking app for graduate students.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The best note-taking app for students.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the target, need, category, benefit and alternative.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Targets by need, states a differentiator a competitor could not claim, supports it with proof, and plans a test.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;For graduate students who read hundreds of papers and lose track of where an idea came from, [the app] is a notebook that links every note to its source passage. Unlike general note apps, where you copy and paste and lose the origin, it keeps the citation attached, so writing a literature review means assembling notes you can trace.</p>\n<p>&quot;What makes it sharp: the target is defined by a need (managing sources), not by 'students'; the benefit is one thing (traceable notes); the differentiator is specific; and a competitor could not say it unless they built it. Proof: a test with ten graduate students writing a short review, measuring time to assemble and citation errors against their current tools. For messaging I'd show three headlines to twenty students and ask which they'd repeat to a labmate and what they think the app does; I'd choose the one they can repeat and understand correctly.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Need-based target; one benefit; a differentiator; proof and a test.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Why not target all students? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A broad target produces a vague message and loses to general tools. I'd start with the segment with the sharpest need and the most willingness to pay, and expand when that works.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if a big competitor adds the same feature? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then the differentiator must be the way it works together: the workflow around sources, the quality of the links and the habit users have built. I'd watch for it and track how much of our retention comes from that feature.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c20-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C20-03 · Intermediate · strategy-gtm · ST · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Price a new premium wearable together with its services subscription.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd price it like competitors, maybe a bit higher because it's premium.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses cost floor, willingness to pay and the subscription's attach and margin.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes the floor, finds the margin-maximizing price from willingness-to-pay data, models services economics over several years, tests a trial, and states the assumption that decides it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; I reason from the structure of device-plus-services businesses and claim nothing about any real product or price. &quot;I'd set the price between a cost floor and the value ceiling and treat the services as a second profit stream. The unit cost is about $200, so a 55% margin gives a floor near $445. Competitors sit between $300 and $500, and the value is a daily health insight. From a willingness-to-pay survey, the margin per respondent is highest at $449 (about 102 against 92 at $349 and 77 at $549), so I'd start at $449 and test $399 and $499, since survey intent overstates purchase.</p>\n<p>&quot;For the subscription at $9.99 a month with a 70% margin, 35% attach and 80% annual renewal, services contribute about $72 per device over three years, which is about 29% on top of the $249 device margin. A three-month free trial that lifts attach to 50% costs about $7 per device to serve and wins by about $13 over three years, but only if attach reaches about 43%; below that it loses. So I'd test the trial on half of buyers and decide on 90-day attach and retention.</p>\n<p>&quot;Positioning: premium on craft and ecosystem value, not price alone; guardrails: returns, services churn and support load.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Floor, WTP-based price, services economics, the deciding assumption, a test.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the competitor cuts price by 20%? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd check whether the segment I'm targeting responds to price: the survey and sales data by segment. If it does not, I'd hold and strengthen the value story; if the survey shows a drop in the share willing at $449, I'd consider an entry model and keep the premium for the flagship.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Would you bundle the subscription into the device price? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Possibly, for the first year, as a way to raise attach and retention, which the trial analysis suggests could pay if attach is high enough. The risk is conditioning customers to expect it free, so I'd make the included period explicit and test it.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c20-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C20-04 · Intermediate · strategy-gtm · ST · Amazon</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How would you decide whether to add a new benefit to a paid membership program?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add it if customers want it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares cost per member with effects on retention and spend.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes the break-even retention gain, accounts for selection and cannibalization, designs a holdout test with guardrails, and considers honest enrollment and cancellation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; I describe a method and no real program's economics <code>Inferred</code>. &quot;I'd compare the benefit's cost per member with its effect on the member's value. Suppose a member pays $120 a year and contributes $150 a year of margin on their spending, so a retained member-year is worth $270, and renewal today is 85%. The benefit costs $18 per member a year. If it lifts renewal by 1.5 points, that is worth 0.015 × $270 = $4.05; if it also raises spending by 2%, that adds $3.00; the total is $7.05 against a cost of $18, a net loss of about $11 per member. To break even the benefit would need to lift renewal by about 5.6 points ((18 − 3) ÷ 270), which is unlikely for one benefit.</p>\n<p>&quot;So I'd not add it as designed, and I'd ask what could change the math: a cheaper way to deliver it, or a benefit that targets a segment where the effect is bigger (members at risk of leaving). I'd test with a holdout, measure renewal and spending at 90 and 365 days, and watch for selection (heavy users take up the benefit most). I'd also check that the program remains honest: clear enrollment and easy cancellation, since regulators and customers care (C10.10).&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Break-even computed; selection; a test; honesty about enrollment.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the benefit also attracts new members? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then I'd add acquisition value: new members times their lifetime contribution, compared with the cost across all members, since existing members pay for the benefit too. The test would measure sign-ups in markets with and without the benefit.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if leadership wants it for competitive reasons? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd show the cost and the break-even, and propose a cheaper version or a narrower launch. If it's a strategic choice, I'd say so explicitly and track the cost as an investment with a review date.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c20-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C20-05 · Intermediate · strategy-gtm · ST · Microsoft</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Should an AI assistant be sold as an add-on to a productivity suite or bundled into the suite price?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Bundle it so everyone uses it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares revenue, cost and adoption for each.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes contribution under each option, includes churn and cost to serve, considers strategic effects (adoption, data, competition), and plans a test.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real product or price <code>Inferred</code>. &quot;I'd compute the contribution of each option for 1,000 users. The suite is $12 per user per month; the assistant costs about $6 per user per month to serve for a full user and $2.40 on average if the average user is lighter. <em>Add-on:</em> priced at $20, 15% attach: revenue 1,000 × $12 + 150 × $20 = $15,000; cost 150 × $6 = $900; contribution $14,100. <em>Bundle:</em> the suite price rises to $17 for everyone; suppose 5% of users leave over the increase: 950 users at $17 is $16,150; cost 950 × $2.40 = $2,280; contribution $13,870. On these numbers the add-on is slightly better; if the price rise lost only 2% of users, the bundle would be about $14,300 and ahead.</p>\n<p>&quot;So the numbers are close, and the deciding factors are strategic: a bundle puts the assistant in front of everyone, which grows usage, feedback and habit, and may defend against competitors; an add-on protects margin and lets heavy users self-select, and reveals willingness to pay. I'd propose an add-on first, with a trial for all, then move to a bundle at a higher suite price if attach and usage show that most users value it. I'd test price and packaging on a sample of enterprise customers, with guardrails on renewal and complaints.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Computed comparison; sensitivity; strategic factors; a staged plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What would make you choose the bundle at once? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;If competitors have bundled and users expect it, or if the assistant's value depends on everyone using it (a shared workspace), or if the cost to serve falls enough that the bundle's margin holds. I'd compute with the new cost.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you handle enterprise customers who negotiate? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With a price list and guardrails: a floor per seat, discounts tied to volume and term, and a rule that the assistant is not given away free to close a suite renewal. Sales needs a clear story and a clear no (C32.7).&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c20-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C20-06 · Intermediate · strategy-gtm · ST · Netflix</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Should a subscription streaming service launch a cheaper, ad-supported tier?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, to reach more subscribers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Weighs new subscribers against cannibalization.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes revenue under cannibalization assumptions, finds the break-even number of new subscribers, considers ad load and retention, and designs a test.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; I reason from the structure of the decision and claim nothing about any real company's numbers <code>Inferred</code>. &quot;The question is whether the new tier adds more revenue than it takes from the premium tier. Suppose 1,000 premium subscribers at $15 a month: revenue $15,000. The ad tier is $8 plus about $5 a month of advertising revenue per subscriber, $13 in total. If 20% of premium subscribers move down and the tier adds 150 new subscribers, revenue is 800 × $15 + 200 × $13 + 150 × $13 = $16,550, up 10%. If 35% move down, it is 650 × $15 + 350 × $13 + 150 × $13 = $16,250. At 35% cannibalization, the tier needs at least 54 new subscribers to break even, about 5.4% growth. So the answer depends on the two uncertain numbers, the share who move down and the number who join.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd also check ad revenue per subscriber, which depends on ad load and on advertisers, and retention: ad-tier subscribers may churn faster or slower; content costs are largely fixed, so the extra subscribers' margin is high. Fences matter: features the premium plan keeps (resolution, downloads, no ads) so that the move-down is limited. I'd test in one market with a holdout, measuring sign-ups, switching and retention at 90 days, with guardrails on satisfaction and ad complaints.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Computed cannibalization and break-even; ad economics; fences; a test.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if ad revenue per subscriber is lower than expected? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then the tier is less attractive: at $3 a month of ads, the tier's revenue per subscriber is $11 and the break-even new-subscriber count rises. I'd raise the price of the ad tier, increase ad load carefully or reduce the premium discount.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you protect the premium plan? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With fences customers value (quality, offline viewing, ad-free), a price gap that is not too wide, and communication that frames the tiers by need. I'd monitor the move-down rate and retain a holdout to measure it.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c20-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C20-07 · Intermediate · strategy-gtm · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Your referral program has a viral coefficient of 0.4. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Try to get it above 1 so it goes viral.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Computes what 0.4 means and what drives it.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes the multiplier and cycle effect, decomposes k into invitations and conversion, evaluates incentives against quality, and sets guardrails and unit economics.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;At k = 0.4 each new user brings 0.4 more, so the loop is not self-sustaining, but it multiplies acquisition by 1 ÷ (1 − 0.4) = 1.67: 10,000 users acquired by other means become about 16,700 over several cycles, and the effective acquisition cost falls by 40%. That is worth having and worth improving; it is not a growth engine by itself.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd decompose k: say 4 invitations per user at 10% conversion. Levers: invitations (make the prompt contextual, at the moment the user gets value, and give a reason to invite: sharing something the recipient will want); conversion (the invitation page and the first-use experience for the invited). Raising invitations to 5 and conversion to 12% gives k = 0.6 and a multiplier of 2.5. An incentive can raise both but may attract people who do not use the product, so I'd test it against a holdout and measure the retention and activation of referred users and the reward cost per retained user.</p>\n<p>&quot;Guardrails: spam complaints and unsubscribes, fraud (fake accounts to earn rewards), and the quality of referred users compared with other channels. Unit economics: if a reward costs $5 and a referred user is worth $30 in contribution, it pays; if referred users churn twice as fast, it may not.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Multiplier; decomposition; incentive tradeoffs; guardrails.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the cycle time is long? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The value arrives late and cash tied up in rewards pays back slowly; I'd reduce the time to the first invitation (invite at activation) and measure cumulative users by week, not just k.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you know k is real? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd measure it by cohort from actual attribution, check that referred users are new (not existing users re-registering), and look at conversion from invitation to active user, not to sign-up.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c20-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C20-08 · Hard · strategy-gtm · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How would you launch a significant new feature to existing users? Give the plan and the success criteria.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Announce it by email and in the app.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A staged launch with a checklist and metrics.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Targets a segment with a message, a launch tier and timeline, a readiness checklist across functions, success criteria with guardrails and rollback triggers, a holdout, and a post-launch review.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;First, who it is for within the base and what problem it solves for them: say a scheduling feature for the 30% of users who book appointments weekly. The message: 'book in two taps instead of six,' with proof from the beta. The tier: a staged rollout with an announcement only after the first stage is stable (C17).</p>\n<p>&quot;Timeline. Eight weeks out: positioning, success metrics and rollout plan agreed. Six weeks: beta with 2,000 users; feedback. Four weeks: documentation, support and sales enablement, in-product messages drafted. Two weeks: a readiness review across product, engineering, design, data, support, legal and marketing, with a checklist (quality, performance, accessibility, privacy, instrumentation, help content, rollback). Launch week: 5%, then 25%, then 100% over a week, with a daily check. Two to four weeks after: a review.</p>\n<p>&quot;Success criteria, written in advance: the share of target users who use the feature at least twice in the first two weeks (primary; target 35%), repeat usage at 30 days, and impact on the core metric (bookings per user) against a 5% holdout; guardrails: support contacts per user, crash rate, and cancellations; rollback if crash rate doubles or bookings per user fall more than 2%. I'd review adoption, retention and the holdout comparison, and decide to expand, iterate or roll back.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> A target and message; a staged plan; cross-functional readiness; criteria and rollback; a holdout.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if adoption is at 15% against a 35% target? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd diagnose before changing: is the problem awareness (users didn't see it), activation (they tried it and left) or value (they used it and it didn't help)? Each has a different fix: better placement and messaging, a simpler first use, or a product change. I'd test the cheapest fix first.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you work with sales and support in the launch? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd give them the value story, a clear scope, known issues and a feedback route, hold a launch review a week before and agree who answers what. Support is the early signal of problems, so I'd review its tickets daily in launch week.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c20-09\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C20-09 · Hard · strategy-gtm · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Customer acquisition cost has risen 40% over two quarters. Diagnose it and decide what to do.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Cut marketing spend.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments by channel and checks conversion.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes the effect on payback and LTV:CAC, decomposes the rise (mix, saturation, conversion, price or product changes), checks cohort quality, and decides with a rule.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;First the effect: CAC rose from $45 to $63. With contribution of $6 a month (ARPU $8, margin 75%), payback went from 7.5 to 10.5 months, and with LTV of $120 the ratio fell from 2.67 to 1.90, below the usual threshold of about 3 <code>[V: arithmetic checked; the 3:1 and 12-month benchmarks are common heuristics, not sourced]</code>. So it matters.</p>\n<p>&quot;Diagnose by decomposing CAC into spend and new customers, by channel: is spend up while volume is flat (saturation: the cheapest audiences are used up and we are buying more expensive ones), did the mix shift toward a costly channel, did conversion fall (a landing page or product change; the funnel step where it fell), did a competitor or an auction raise prices, or is attribution broken (a tracking change counts fewer customers)? I'd also check the quality: do new customers retain as well as before? A cheap but poor channel and an expensive but good one look different in CAC and the same in value.</p>\n<p>&quot;Then decide with a rule: for each channel compute LTV:CAC and payback; cut or cap channels below the threshold; shift spend to channels that pass; fix the funnel step that fell if that is the cause; and improve retention if LTV is the lever. I would not cut across the board: it may drop the good channels with the bad.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> The effect on payback and ratio; decomposition; cohort quality; a channel-level rule.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you tell saturation from a funnel problem? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Saturation shows as rising cost per click and falling reach in the same audience with a stable conversion rate; a funnel problem shows as a stable cost per click and a falling conversion at a particular step. Splitting CAC into those two parts tells which.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the finance team wants CAC back to $45 this quarter? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd show the channel-level view and propose which channels to cut and which to keep, with the payback of each, and what each choice does to volume. A target met by cutting good channels would reduce growth, which they may not want.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c20-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Eight exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Every figure is invented.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c20-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C20-01 · A GTM one-pager</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Fill in a go-to-market one-pager (FW-C20-01) for &quot;a feature that lets restaurant owners accept table reservations in a delivery app.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Strategy practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C20-01.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Target and problem:</em> independent restaurant owners with 10 to 60 seats who take reservations by phone and lose bookings when they are busy. <em>Value and positioning:</em> &quot;never miss a booking: customers reserve in the app you already use; no new tool&quot; against the phone and a standalone booking tool. <em>Price and packaging:</em> free for the first 50 reservations a month, then $29 a month for unlimited; a no-show deposit option as an add-on. <em>Motion and channels:</em> in-app prompts to existing delivery partners (product-led), plus sales calls for chains. <em>Launch:</em> a beta with 200 restaurants in two cities, then a staged rollout. <em>Success:</em> share of beta restaurants taking at least 20 reservations a month by week four; guardrails: no-show rate, support contacts, delivery orders. <em>Owners:</em> PM, product marketing, partner sales, support, finance.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Start from the competing tool and write the difference.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A narrow target for the beta gives cleaner learning.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c20-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C20-02 · A positioning statement and test</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write a positioning statement for a budgeting app for freelancers with irregular income and design a test of two messages.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Positioning practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C20-02.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> &quot;For freelancers whose income changes every month and who struggle to know what they can safely spend, [the app] is a budgeting tool that sets aside tax and a buffer from each payment automatically. Unlike spreadsheets and fixed-income budgeting apps, it plans around uneven months.&quot; Test: two headlines, &quot;Budget for irregular income&quot; and &quot;Know what's safe to spend this week,&quot; shown to 300 freelancers each in a landing-page test; the measure is sign-up conversion with 30-day activation as a guardrail (to avoid choosing a headline that attracts the wrong users); also ask ten freelancers to say in their own words what the app does.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Test the proof element (a number about taxes saved) instead of the headline.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A narrow message wins the target and may repel others; that is its purpose.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c20-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C20-03 · Floor and willingness to pay</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A device costs $120 to make and deliver. Target margin 60%. A survey of 500 shows willingness to buy of 55% at $199, 40% at $249, 24% at $299 and 10% at $349. Compute the floor, the margin per respondent at each price and the starting price.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Pricing.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C20-03 and Worked example 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Floor: $120 ÷ (1 − 0.60) = $300. Margin per respondent: $199 gives $79 × 0.55 = 43.5; $249 gives $129 × 0.40 = 51.6; $299 gives $179 × 0.24 = 43.0; $349 gives $229 × 0.10 = 22.9. The margin is highest at $249, but that price is below the $300 floor, so the 60% margin target fails there (the margin is 52%). If the target is firm, price at $299 (a 59.9% margin, effectively at the floor) or $349 and accept lower volume; if volume matters more, price at $249 and accept the lower margin. State the tradeoff, test at $249 and $299, and decide on revenue, margin and returns. Survey intent overstates purchase, so use it for relative comparison.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Lower the unit cost: a $100 cost gives a floor of $250 and fits the margin-maximizing price.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A margin target and a volume goal can conflict; make the choice explicit.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c20-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C20-04 · The bundle test</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Three customers value products A and B (at zero marginal cost) as follows: customer 1, A $12, B $2; customer 2, A $7, B $7; customer 3, A $2, B $12. Compute the best separate prices and revenue, and the revenue from a bundle at $14.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Bundling.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C20-04 and Worked example 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Separate: A at $7 sells to customers 1 and 2, revenue $14 (at $12 only customer 1: $12; at $2 all three: $6); B at $7 sells to customers 2 and 3, revenue $14; total $28. Bundle: each customer values the pair at $14; at $14 all three buy: revenue $42, 50% higher than $28. The valuations are negatively correlated (who values A most values B least), so the bundle fits. Check marginal costs and cannibalization before deciding.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Give the third customer a value of $12 for both products; separate pricing would then match the bundle, and bundling would not help.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A bundle can hide the price of each part; check perceived fairness.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c20-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C20-05 · A referral loop</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Each user sends 5 invitations and 10% convert. Compute k and the multiplier. What change gets k to 0.8? Compute the total from 8,000 starting users at k = 0.5 and k = 0.8.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Growth loops.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C20-05 and Worked example 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> k = 5 × 0.10 = 0.5; multiplier 1 ÷ (1 − 0.5) = 2. To reach 0.8: 8 invitations at 10%, or 5 at 16%, or 6 at about 13.3%. Total from 8,000 users: at k = 0.5, 8,000 × 2 = 16,000; at k = 0.8, 8,000 × 5 = 40,000 (long-run, over many cycles; the cycle time sets how fast). Conversion is usually more valuable to improve than the number of invitations, since more invitations annoy users. Guardrails: referred-user quality, spam complaints and reward cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Compute the users after three cycles at k = 0.8: 8,000 × (1 + 0.8 + 0.64 + 0.512) = 23,616.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Higher k from incentives may reduce user quality.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c20-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C20-06 · Unit economics</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> ARPU $12, gross margin 80%, monthly churn 4%, CAC $90. Compute contribution, LTV, payback and the ratio. What churn is needed for a ratio of 3 at this CAC?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Acquisition economics.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C20-06 and Worked example 5.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Contribution: $12 × 0.8 = $9.60 a month. LTV: $9.60 ÷ 0.04 = $240. Ratio: 240 ÷ 90 = 2.67. Payback: 90 ÷ 9.60 = 9.4 months. For a ratio of 3 at CAC $90, LTV must be $270, so churn = $9.60 ÷ $270 = 3.56% a month. Alternatively CAC must fall to $80. Decision: payback is acceptable; improve retention by about half a point or lower CAC by about $10 before scaling spend, and check churn by cohort.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Compute the ratio at 5% churn: LTV $192, ratio 2.13.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A longer payback is acceptable with cash and confidence in churn; a shorter one is safer.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c20-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C20-07 · A launch plan</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write a launch plan (FW-C20-07) for a new &quot;collaborative playlists&quot; feature in a music app: tier, timeline, checklist, success criteria with guardrails and a rollback trigger.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Launch planning.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C20-07 and IQ-C20-08.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Tier:</em> a staged rollout with an in-app announcement after stability. <em>Timeline:</em> eight weeks out, positioning and metrics; six weeks, beta with 5,000 users; four weeks, help content, support and marketing materials; two weeks, readiness review (quality, performance, accessibility, privacy, content-moderation rules for shared lists, instrumentation, rollback); launch week 5%, 25%, 100%; after launch, a four-week review. <em>Success:</em> the share of weekly active users who create or join a collaborative playlist within two weeks (target 12%), repeat use at 30 days and effect on weekly listening hours against a 5% holdout. <em>Guardrails:</em> reports of inappropriate content, support contacts, crash rate. <em>Rollback trigger:</em> crash rate doubling, or reports rising above a stated threshold, or listening hours falling more than 1%.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Limit the beta to friend groups, where the feature's value is clearest.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A longer beta delays the launch and reduces risk.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c20-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C20-08 · Pricing for a local market</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A global app charges $12 a month. In a market where average income and willingness to pay are much lower, test price points. Suppose a local price of 40% of the global price ($4.80), a monthly cost to serve of $1.20 per user, and a local payment fee of 4%. Compute the margin, and list three design changes for payments and plans.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Regional lens (India).</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The Regional Lens in C20, and C09.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Revenue $4.80; payment fee 4% = $0.19; cost to serve $1.20; contribution $4.80 − $0.19 − $1.20 = $3.41 a month (71%). So the local price remains profitable at 40% of the global price. Design changes: (1) offer the payment methods people actually use (bank-transfer payments through UPI, wallets and cards) and design renewals around recurring-payment mandate rules, including authentication limits (waived up to 15,000 rupees, and up to 1,00,000 rupees for some categories) <code>[V: RBI circular RBI/2023-24/88 of 12 December 2023; e-mandate framework of August 2019; the same limits appear in RBI's later notifications, including its 2025 authentication directions, checked October 2026]</code>; (2) offer shorter or smaller plans (weekly or monthly, lower-priced tiers, prepaid options) instead of annual-only; (3) support local languages and low-data use. Test two or three local price points on new users with guardrails on retention and refunds, and check for arbitrage (users in other markets buying at the local price).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Test a lower-priced ad-supported or limited tier if willingness to pay is lower still.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Lower prices raise reach and lower revenue per user; check the unit economics for the new price.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for two weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: price and position.</em> Use IQ-C20-01, IQ-C20-02, IQ-C20-03, EX-C20-02, EX-C20-03 and EX-C20-04. Answer &quot;How would you price this?&quot; aloud in twenty minutes for three products, and fill in a pricing worksheet for each.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: growth, economics and launch.</em> Use IQ-C20-07, IQ-C20-08, IQ-C20-09, EX-C20-05, EX-C20-06 and EX-C20-07. Compute k, LTV and payback by hand and write a launch plan with success criteria and a rollback trigger.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to say &quot;A competitor charges half&quot; and &quot;Won't this cannibalize our main plan?&quot; at random, and to ask for the number behind every claim.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I start from the customer and the value, not from a price?</li>\n<li>Did I give a cost floor, a reference and a value estimate with numbers?</li>\n<li>Did I choose the model, the value metric and the tiers, and say what each tier is for?</li>\n<li>Did I propose a test with guardrails and a decision rule?</li>\n<li>Did I compute CAC, LTV, payback and a sensitivity?</li>\n<li>For a loop, did I compute k, the multiplier and the cycle time?</li>\n<li>Did my launch plan include success criteria, guardrails and a rollback trigger?</li>\n<li>Did I say what would change my recommendation?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> Record the date, the prompt, the number you were asked for and whether you computed it, your score and the one fix.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter on real work over the next three weeks. If you do not work on pricing or launches, apply it to a product you use.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Pick a product or feature (yours or one you use) and write its go-to-market one-pager (FW-C20-01) and positioning statement (FW-C20-02). Ask product marketing or a colleague what is missing.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Fill in a pricing worksheet (FW-C20-03) for it: floor, reference, value, model, value metric, tiers, test plan. If pricing exists, compare the current price with the worksheet and note the gap.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Compute the acquisition economics (FW-C20-06) from real data if you have it: ARPU, margin, churn by cohort, CAC by channel; build the sensitivity grid. If there is a referral or sharing feature, compute k and the multiplier (FW-C20-05).</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3.</strong> Write the launch plan (FW-C20-07) for the next release: tier, checklist, timeline, success criteria and rollback trigger; hold a launch review with product marketing, support and engineering a week before.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the pricing worksheet with a test result (a time you priced from evidence), the economics sheet with a decision (a time you used LTV and payback), and the launch plan and review (a time you coordinated a launch). Keep numbers real and credit partners.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c20-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Go-to-market is the plan for how a product reaches and wins its customers: target, value, positioning, price and packaging, channels, launch and metrics; the PM owns the problem and the value, co-owns positioning and pricing, and carries the launch with product marketing, sales, support and finance.</li>\n<li>Price between a cost floor and a value ceiling using competitor references and willingness-to-pay evidence, choose a value metric that tracks both value and cost, and test the price on real traffic with guardrails; cost-plus is a floor, not a method, and bundles help when valuations differ and are negatively correlated.</li>\n<li>Distinguish funnels from loops: compute the viral coefficient, the multiplier N ÷ (1 − k) and the cycle time, and remember that incentives can raise k and lower quality; most products need other acquisition besides virality.</li>\n<li>Read unit economics with care: contribution = ARPU × margin, LTV ≈ contribution ÷ churn, payback = CAC ÷ contribution, a ratio near 3 and payback under about a year as a heuristic, by channel and by cohort, with a sensitivity grid; fix retention before scaling spend when the ratio is low.</li>\n<li>Plan a launch with a tier, a cross-functional readiness checklist, a timeline, success criteria with guardrails and a rollback trigger, and a holdout; plan the transition for existing customers when you change a price or a model, as Adobe's 2013 move shows.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c20-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Adobe's press release of 6 May 2013; its quarterly report for the period ended 31 May 2013; its earnings releases for the third and fourth quarters of fiscal 2013; and its annual reports for fiscal 2011, 2012 and 2014, as filed with the US Securities and Exchange Commission. Sources for CS-C20-02 (confirmed in October 2026).</li>\n<li>Thomas Nagle and Georg Müller, <em>The Strategy and Tactics of Pricing</em> (first edition 1987; 6th edition 2018; a 7th edition appeared in 2024), for the standard treatment of value-based pricing <code>[V: first edition 1987; sixth edition Routledge, 2018 (ISBN 9781138737518); a seventh edition with Evert Gruyaert followed in 2024]</code>.</li>\n<li>Peter van Westendorp's price sensitivity meter (ESOMAR Congress paper, 1976), as described in pricing research texts <code>[V: van Westendorp, NSS Price Sensitivity Meter, Proceedings of the 29th ESOMAR Congress, 1976, pp. 139 to 167]</code>.</li>\n<li>Andrew Chen, <em>The Cold Start Problem</em> (2021), on growth loops and network effects <code>[V: Harper Business, 7 December 2021, ISBN 9780062969743]</code>.</li>\n<li>Patrick Campbell (founder of ProfitWell, formerly Price Intelligently, acquired by Paddle in 2022) and others on subscription pricing and packaging, and company pricing pages as primary evidence of how real products are packaged, read with the date in mind <code>[V: ProfitWell (formerly Price Intelligently), founded 2012 by Patrick Campbell, acquired by Paddle in May 2022]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"part-vii\"></span>\n";
